+      <article class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>A good cup begins long before the roaster warms up. It starts with the soil, the season, and the people who tend the coffee trees. At The Daily Grind, our sourcing process begins with a simple question: what makes this coffee worth sharing? We look for beans with a clear sense of place, whether that means a bright, fruit-forward cup or something rounder, with the comforting sweetness of chocolate and toasted nuts. Flavor opens the conversation, but it is only the beginning of the story.</p>
+
+        <p>Before choosing a coffee, we ask our sourcing partners about the farm or cooperative behind it, when the cherries were harvested, and how they were processed. Those details help us understand both the character of the beans and the work that went into them. We value partners who can explain how a lot moves from grower to exporter and who are willing to discuss pricing, working conditions, and the challenges of the harvest. Our aim is to build lasting relationships around transparency, rather than making a decision from a tasting note alone.</p>
+
+        <p>We also ask how producers care for the land that supports their crop. Shade trees, healthy soil, careful water use, and thoughtful handling of processing waste all belong in that conversation. There is no single recipe that fits every growing region: a dry hillside and a rainy valley call for different approaches. Listening to producers helps us understand those differences and consider environmental practices in their local context. We see sourcing as an ongoing exchange, with more to learn each season.</p>
+
+        <p>Next comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and cup them side by side, paying attention to aroma, sweetness, acidity, body, and the finish that lingers after a sip. We taste more than once because a coffee should offer balance and consistency as well as an exciting first impression. When a lot arrives, we compare it with the original sample and check its condition before planning a production roast. Careful storage and manageable buying quantities help us protect the qualities we chose it for.</p>
+
+        <p>Finally, we develop a roast that brings those qualities into focus. We adjust time and heat in small steps, then taste again, looking for a profile that lets the origin speak while making a satisfying everyday cup. Our sourcing work continues after the beans reach the shelf: we keep notes, listen to feedback, and use what we learn to guide the next purchase. Every bag is one chapter in a longer relationship between the grower, our roasting team, and the person brewing it. That is the journey we want you to taste in your morning coffee.</p>
+      </article>
